---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHero from "../../components/PageHero.astro";
import AboutVolumeAtlas from "../../components/AboutVolumeAtlas.astro";
import { sitePath } from "../../lib/paths";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Mission and editorial principles of Independent Observer."
  currentPath="/about/"
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="About the project"
    title="Independence is a method, not a pose."
    description="When public life rewards reflex, spectacle, and retaliation, independent observation creates a disciplined pause: document the record before joining the reaction. Volume I asks how time, resources, institutional access, and economic room shape what becomes visible, then carries that method through power, work, technology, and human capability."
    className="about-hero"
  />
  <section class="section section-navy" aria-labelledby="volume-one-method-title">
    <div class="shell page-grid">
      <aside class="page-aside">
        <span>Four volumes</span>
        <span>One method</span>
        <span>Volume I · Method</span>
      </aside>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="volume-one-method-title">Observe before you react—and connect the four volumes.</h2>
        <p class="sr-only">Observe before you react.</p>
        <p>
          Independent observation is a pause between stimulus and certainty. Volume I establishes
          the discipline: name the source, date the record, separate fact from interpretation, and
          keep uncertainty visible. Its public reading anchor is the <a
            href={sitePath("/library/documents/independent-observer-volume-one-ssrn/")}
            >Foundational Manifesto</a
          >.
        </p>
        <p>
          The method then moves through the series. Volume II, <em>The Empire Beneath Democracy</em
          >, studies sovereignty, institutions, migration, enforcement, and geopolitical dependence.
          Volume III, <em>Managed Decline</em>, follows work, time, taxation, welfare, and social
          citizenship—including <em>The Wardhan Tax Doctrine</em>. Volume IV, <em
            >The Last Human Workforce</em
          >, tests how AI, science, infrastructure, intimacy, and human capability become usable
          public capacity.
        </p>
        <p>
          They are not four disconnected brands or four conclusions. They are a sequence: Volume I
          makes observation accountable; Volume II locates the power that shapes the record; Volume
          III asks who bears the cost; and Volume IV asks who can govern the capabilities that
          follow. Each volume returns to the same question—what would make public life more legible
          and more correctable?
        </p>
        <p>
          Volume I keeps documented fact distinct from interpretation and hypothesis. The archive’s
          Charles Hamilton Houston example illustrates the same principle: build a record before
          turning it into an argument.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href={sitePath("/series/independent-observer/")}>Read the Volume I catalogue file →</a>
          <span aria-hidden="true"> · </span>
          <a href="#about-volume-atlas">Browse the interactive volume atlas →</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section section-white">
    <div class="shell page-grid">
      <aside class="page-aside">Mission</aside>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Look past the approved frame.</h2>
        <p>
          Public controversies are often presented as isolated disputes between personalities or
          parties. This project instead asks how rules, incentives, historical memory, technology,
          and institutional structure shape the available choices.
        </p>
        <p>
          Independent Observer is a connected four-volume inquiry. Each volume carries the same
          method into a different part of public life, so the project can examine not only what
          happened, but the conditions that made it visible, durable, or difficult to change.
        </p>
        <p>
          Read the four volumes as a sequence: Volume I establishes how to observe; Volume II asks
          where authority and sovereignty are organized; Volume III examines work, taxation, and
          social citizenship; Volume IV tests technological change against human capability and asks
          who gets to govern it.
        </p>
        <p>
          “Independent” does not mean free from values, error, or perspective. It means the work
          should not conceal its reasoning behind tribal loyalty. Claims should be sourced,
          interpretations should be identifiable, and uncertainty should remain visible.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <AboutVolumeAtlas />
</BaseLayout>
